course: dictionary
lesson: 13040
title: Fanned: moving air and spreading out
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Fanned is the past tense of fan, so it tells you something has already happened. Look at the words around it to find its meaning. «She fanned herself with a menu on the hot day.» The hot day tells you she moved air towards herself to cool down.
explain: Fan can also mean making a fire burn more strongly. «The wind fanned the campfire, and the sparks flew up.» The fire is the clue here: the wind blew air onto it, so the flames grew.
explain: Fanned can mean spread out from one point, like the folds of an open fan. «The searchers fanned out across the wood.» They did not all go the same way. They spread out to cover more ground.
explain: Fanned can also be used for a feeling. «His cruel words fanned the flames of anger.» Flames are fire, so the words made the anger burn stronger. Try your meaning in the sentence: does it still make sense? If yes, you have got it.
fact: Fanned is the past tense of fan, so it tells you something happened in the past.
fact: «Fanned» can mean moved air towards something to cool it, or made a fire burn more strongly.
fact: «Fanned out» means spread out from one point, like an open fan.
fact: «Fanned the flames» means made a strong feeling, like anger, even stronger.
fact: In Alice in Wonderland, Alice fans herself with the White Rabbit's fan and starts to shrink.
exercise: «She fanned herself with a menu on the hot day.» What did she do? | Shouted for help | Put out a fire | *Moved air towards herself to cool down | Walked away quickly
exercise: «The searchers fanned out across the wood.» What does fanned out mean here? | Lost their way in the trees | Stood still in a straight line | Ran back home together | *Spread out from one point
exercise: «The wind fanned the campfire, and it blazed.» What did the wind do to the fire? | Put it out completely | Turned it into smoke only | Moved it to the other side of the field | *Made it burn more strongly
test: «Dad fanned the pizza with a tea towel to cool it.» What does fanned mean here? | *Moved air towards it to cool it down | Dropped it on the floor | Heated it up in the oven
test: «His cruel words fanned the flames of anger.» What did the words do to the anger? | *Made it grow stronger | Made him laugh | Hid it from everyone | Stopped it at once
exam: What is the past tense of fan? | fans | fan | fanning | *fanned
